        "lessons.w12.general1.hard": "A volcano is an opening in the Earth's crust where molten rock, gases and ash come out. Underground, the molten rock is called magma; once it reaches the surface, it is called lava.",
        "lessons.w12.general1.easy": "A volcano is a place where melted rock from deep underground comes out. Underground it's called magma, and when it comes out, it's called lava.",
        "lessons.w12.general1.like": "Magma rises because it is lighter than the solid rock around it, a bit like a blob of oil rising in water.",
        "lessons.w12.general2.hard": "Most active volcanoes lie along the edges of tectonic plates, and many of them form the “Ring of Fire” around the Pacific Ocean.",
        "lessons.w12.general2.easy": "The Earth's surface is broken into giant pieces called plates. Most volcanoes are where plates meet, and lots of them circle the Pacific Ocean: the “Ring of Fire”.",
        "lessons.w12.general2.like": "The plates fit together like the pieces of a cracked eggshell, and they move about as fast as your fingernails grow.",
        "lessons.w12.general3.hard": "There are several types: broad shield volcanoes with gentle slopes, small cinder cones, and tall, steep stratovolcanoes built from layers of lava and ash.",
        "lessons.w12.general3.easy": "Volcanoes come in different shapes: some are wide with gentle slopes, some are small cones, and some are tall and steep.",
        "lessons.w12.general3.like": "A shield volcano is shaped like a warrior's shield lying on the ground; a stratovolcano is built up layer by layer, like a layer cake.",
        "lessons.w12.focused1.hard": "Whether an eruption is explosive depends mainly on the magma's viscosity (how thick and sticky it is) and on how much gas is dissolved in it.",
        "lessons.w12.focused1.easy": "It mostly depends on two things: how thick and sticky the melted rock is, and how much gas is trapped inside it.",
        "lessons.w12.focused1.like": "Runny magma is like warm syrup; sticky magma is more like toothpaste.",
        "lessons.w12.focused2.hard": "Magma rich in silica is very viscous, so gas bubbles can't escape. The pressure builds up until the magma bursts apart in an explosion of ash and rock.",
        "lessons.w12.focused2.easy": "In thick, sticky magma, the gas can't get out. The pressure grows and grows until it all blasts out at once.",
        "lessons.w12.focused2.like": "It's like shaking a bottle of fizzy drink and then opening it: the gas rushes out and takes the drink with it.",
